{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-164148",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}((x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2})\\frac{x}{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}x(x-2)^{2}+x(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[x(x-2)^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x(y-3)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}6x(x-2)^{2}+18x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}6{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+42x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+21{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{243}{2}-216+189-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{189}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
